---
// ===========================================================================
// runtime.astro — what a write touches, and when a verdict happens.
//
// The demo comes before the prose because it is better evidence than the
// prose. Every figure here was produced by running the runtime with a counting
// adapter, and a reader can reproduce two of them by typing.
// ===========================================================================
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Code from "../components/Code.astro";
import WriteFlow from "../components/WriteFlow.astro";
import LiveForm from "../demo/LiveForm.tsx";

const writing = `
form.field("owner.name").setValue("Grace");

form.rows("items").insert(2, { sku: "" });
form.rows("items").remove(0);
form.rows("items").move(0, 2);

form.reset();                 // back to the defaults it was built with
await form.submit(save);      // judges first; hands over only if nothing blocks
`;

const TRY = [
  [
    "Type in name",
    "Only that row's counter moves. The other four do not render at all.",
  ],
  [
    "Change billing",
    "The error lands on shipping, a field you did not touch. One pass judges the whole root and the diff writes wherever the verdict moved.",
  ],
  [
    "Switch to useUncontrolledField, then type a valid value",
    "The tape keeps printing and the counter does not move at all. Same cell, subscribed imperatively, writing the DOM node instead of re-rendering.",
  ],
  [
    "Watch validator passes",
    "One per settled change, however many fields that change touched.",
  ],
  [
    "Type a second character into billing",
    "The tape adds a root and a value and nothing else: the verdict did not move, so no issues cell is written and the row showing the error does not render.",
  ],
  [
    "Add a line, then remove the first row",
    "The tape shows B-2's value moving down a slot and the removed row's cells being forgotten. A value follows its row, because a row is addressed by identity rather than by index.",
  ],
];

const woken = [
  ["Writing the same value again", "nobody", "The store gates an Object.is-equal write."],
  [
    "A different value, same verdict",
    "that field's value",
    "Its issues cell is not written, so an error display beside it does not re-render.",
  ],
  [
    "A value that becomes invalid",
    "that field's value and issues",
    "The pass judged four hundred fields; it woke the one whose error moved.",
  ],
  ["Any of the above", "never another field", "Unless a rule actually moved that field's issues."],
];
---

<Base
  title="The runtime — waypoint"
  description="A live form you can type into, with a render counter on every row. One coalesced whole-root pass per settled change, and a verdict scattered back only onto the paths whose issues actually moved."
>
  <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold sm:text-4xl">How a value changes</h1>

  <p class="lead">
    <strong class="text-on-surface">A keystroke wakes the row it landed in,
    plus the row an error moved to — no other field row renders.</strong>{" "}
    Nothing is lifted and there is no dependency array: values live in a flat,
    path-keyed cell space outside React, and a component subscribes to the
    cells it reads.
  </p>

  <LiveForm client:load />

  <p class="caption">
    The panel under the form is the cell store narrating itself. A recording
    store is handed to <code>createForm</code> — the same option an application
    uses to keep a form in zustand — so every write is printed, newest first.
    A keystroke writes the root and one value; an <code>issues</code> line
    appears only on the pass where a verdict actually moved.
  </p>

  <div class="mt-6 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
    {
      TRY.map(([what, why]) => (
        <div class="rounded-lg bg-surface-low p-4">
          <p class="text-sm font-medium">{what}</p>
          <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">{why}</p>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <h2 class="mt-16 text-2xl font-bold">The same runtime, on your machine</h2>

  <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    Nothing is published, so there is no sandbox to link to — you clone it. Both
    examples resolve the package to its <strong>source</strong>, so an edit
    inside <code>src/core</code> reloads the page with no build step
    in between.
  </p>

  <Code
    code={`git clone https://github.com/maroonedog/form-contract
cd form-contract && npm install

npm run example:showcase        # 23 inputs, six sections, one screen
npm run example:nested-arrays   # the same shape in four libraries, side by side
npm run docs:dev                # this site, with the form above`}
    label="shell"
  />

  <h2 class="mt-16 text-2xl font-bold">Typing that costs React nothing</h2>

  <div class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="w-full text-sm">
      <thead class="text-left text-on-surface-variant">
        <tr class="hairline">
          <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">per keystroke, 201 fields</th>
          <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">verdict does not move</th>
          <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">the field becomes wrong</th>
          <th class="py-2 font-medium">a rule reports elsewhere</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="tabular-nums">
        <tr class="hairline">
          <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-xs">useField</td>
          <td class="py-3 pr-4">1 commit, 212 fibers</td>
          <td class="py-3 pr-4">2 commits, 424</td>
          <td class="py-3">2 commits, 424</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="hairline">
          <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-xs">useUncontrolledField</td>
          <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-primary">0 commits, 0</td>
          <td class="py-3 pr-4">1 commit, 212</td>
          <td class="py-3">1 commit, 212</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="caption">
    That zero is parity with react-hook-form's <code>register</code>, not a win
    over it. The price, and why it is a second hook rather than the default: an
    uncontrolled input cannot be transformed as it is typed. Masking,
    upper-casing and inserting separators all need the value to come back
    through React, which is what <code>useField</code> is for.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-16 text-2xl font-bold">What one keystroke does</h2>

  <WriteFlow />

  <h2 class="mt-16 text-2xl font-bold">Writing</h2>

  <Code code={writing} label="every write goes through a handle" />

  <p class="caption">
    No <code>setState</code>, and no form object to spread.
  </p>

  <p
    class="mt-8 rounded-lg border border-outline-variant p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant"
  >
    What asks for a pass, and what does not — and what one costs — is on{" "}
    <a class="text-primary underline underline-offset-4" href="/validation/"
      >Validation</a
    >.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-16 text-2xl font-bold">Who gets woken</h2>

  <div class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="w-full text-sm">
      <thead class="text-left text-on-surface-variant">
        <tr class="hairline">
          <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">The write</th>
          <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">Woken</th>
          <th class="py-2 font-medium">Note</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          woken.map(([what, who, note]) => (
            <tr class="hairline">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 align-top leading-relaxed">{what}</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 align-top font-mono text-xs">{who}</td>
              <td class="py-3 align-top leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
                {note}
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="caption">
    The pass still runs: <code>setValue</code> always asks for one.
  </p>

  <p class="caption">
    Every figure here came from running the runtime with a counting adapter
    around the real zod resolver.
  </p>
</Base>
